 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "Four hundred people danced in the street for weeks, till the blood was squishing out of their shoes."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "Now hold on, buddy. This ain't some tall tale to put the young'uns to sleep."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "It's written down in every history book. They called it the Dancing Plague."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "So sit tight, and listen how this whole circus got started. Strasbourg, the year fifteen eighteen."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Regular summer day in July. A lady named Frau Troffea busts out of her house, plants herself in the middle of the street, and starts dancing."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "All by her lonesome. No fiddler, no music, not one drop of moonshine in her."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Just hopping around like she sat on a hornet's nest."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "The neighbours peek out the window and go: well, I reckon her husband finally ran off, and now she's celebrating."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Except she didn't dance for five minutes. No sir."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "She danced day and night. No sleep, hardly a bite to eat, for almost a whole week."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Her shoes fell to pieces. Her feet were bleeding. She was bawling that she couldn't stop. But them legs just kept on going."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "And here comes the part that'll curl your toes."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "A few days later, other folks started hopping right next to her."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "Within a week, there were more than thirty. And within a month, some say four hundred people were jumping around that town square, like a barn full of loons."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "And buddy, they weren't dancing 'cause the price of bread went down."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "They were hollering, begging for help. But their arms and legs were flapping all on their own, like the devil himself had moved in."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "And you know what the doctors and the big shots at city hall came up with, to fix it?"
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "They said the only cure was to let 'em dance, till the sickness shook right out of their bones."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "So they built a big wooden stage, and paid musicians with drums and pipes to play non-stop, to keep 'em in rhythm."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "Lord, how dumb can you be?"
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "That's like watching your barn burn down, and throwing more firewood on it, so the fire gets done quicker."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "Well of course, it went south."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "In that summer heat, starving and worn to the bone, people started dropping like flies."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "Chroniclers wrote that some of them died. One even claimed fifteen a day, though historians still argue about that number."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "But why in tarnation did this happen?"
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "There are two big theories."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "Number one: their rye bread might've been rotten with a poison fungus, called ergot."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "That fungus carries the same stuff they cook L S D from today. Gives you spasms and hallucinations, like seeing devils dancing on the walls."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "Number two, and most historians bet on this one: those folks were starving, scared half to death, and they truly believed Saint Vitus could curse you to dance till you dropped."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "So between the bad bread and all that fear, they plumb lost their marbles, and hopped around for two whole months."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "In the end, they hauled the dancers up a mountain, to a shrine of Saint Vitus, to pray. And slowly, it stopped."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "You get it, buddy? I said, you get it?"
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "So next time you whine your feet hurt after two hours at a wedding, think about them poor souls who danced two months straight, some of 'em all the way to the graveyard."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "That's real."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "That's about it, folks. Follow for more strange history. See ya!"
  }
 ],
 "science": [
  {
   "claim": "July 1518, Strasbourg: Frau Troffea danced in the street for days; about 34 joined within a week; chroniclers claim up to 400 within a month; the council prescribed more dancing (guild halls, a wooden stage, paid musicians); some deaths reported, '15 a day' is one later claim and disputed; dancers taken to a shrine of St Vitus near Saverne; the outbreak faded by September.",
   "working": "July to early September is about two months.",
   "source": "Wikipedia, Dancing plague of 1518; John Waller, A Time to Dance, a Time to Die (2008); Smithsonian; History.com"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Ergot (Claviceps purpurea) on rye produces ergot alkaloids, including lysergic acid, the precursor of LSD; ergotism causes convulsions and hallucinations. Many historians (e.g. Waller) favour mass psychogenic illness driven by famine, disease and belief in St Vitus' curse.",
   "working": "",
   "source": "Waller 2008; Britannica, ergotism"
  }
 ],
